     "type": "application",
     "title": "Run the Seven-Question Audit on Your Own Idea",
     "content": "Turn the checklist into a working document, not just a mental exercise.\n\n1. **Write one honest paragraph per question.** No bullet-point shortcuts — force yourself to actually argue the case for each of the seven, including the ones you'd rather skip.\n\n2. **Flag your weakest one or two answers without excuses.** Every real business has at least one shaky answer; the danger is pretending it isn't shaky.\n\n3. **Design one experiment that specifically tests the weakest answer.** If distribution is weak, that means getting one real paying customer before writing another line of code — not writing a better slide about your distribution 'strategy.'\n\n4. **Ask someone who wants you to fail to read your answers.** A skeptical outside reader will find the gaps your own optimism papers over — a cofounder's mother, a former competitor, a blunt mentor.\n\n5. **Revisit all seven answers every quarter**, not just at fundraising time. Markets shift, competitors launch, and an answer that was strong six months ago (say, timing) can quietly become the weakest link without anyone noticing until it's too late to fix cheaply.",
